NAME
    http-fetch - fetch HTTP or HTTPS content through an available host backend

SYNOPSIS
    http-fetch [-H <header>]... [-l] [-o <file>] [-t <seconds>] -- <url>

DESCRIPTION
    http-fetch performs one HTTP or HTTPS request. It prefers curl and falls
    back to GNU Wget when necessary.

    By default the response body is written to standard output. Redirects are
    followed and redirected protocols remain restricted to HTTP and HTTPS.

    With -o, when standard error is connected to a terminal, http-fetch leaves
    the selected backend's native transfer progress visible on standard error.
    Progress is suppressed when standard error is not a terminal, when the
    response body is written to standard output, and for -l requests. Progress
    formatting is backend-specific.

OPTIONS
    -H <header>
        Add one HTTP request header. May be repeated. The value must contain a
        colon and must not contain CR or LF.
    -l
        Request headers and write exactly one valid Content-Length value from
        the final response. Cannot be combined with -o.
    -o <file>
        Write the response body to the specified file.
    -t <seconds>
        Set the positive integer transfer/connectivity timeout. Default: 30.

OPERANDS
    <url>
        One http:// or https:// URL after the mandatory -- delimiter.

EXIT STATUS
    0   Success.
    1   Transfer, backend or response-validation failure.
    2   Invalid invocation.
